    YC-036.6: Build Real Web Fundamentals Content
    
    Replace the Web Fundamentals module's three empty lessons with genuine,
    technically accurate content built around the request/response
    communication flow rather than definitions: URL anatomy and which parts
    the server actually sees, the DNS -> TCP -> TLS -> HTTP chain built
    directly on Computer Networking's DNS/TCP content, client/server vs.
    browser/web server, HTTP request/response format, methods and their
    conventional semantics, status-code families with a worked
    401-vs-403-vs-404 comparison, headers, request/response bodies
    (form-encoded vs. JSON), cookies and the Set-Cookie/Cookie session flow,
    cookie security attributes, authentication vs. authorization made
    concrete with real evidence, why authorization must be server-side, JSON
    APIs including two different real auth mechanisms (cookie session vs.
    bearer token) on the same server, HTTPS/TLS explained conceptually, and
    a malformed-JSON debugging exercise.
    
    Every request/response example was captured by actually running
    app/core/terminal/web.py's WebApp simulator (build_request +
    WebApp.handle) and copying its real output verbatim - not invented
    example data. The simulated site (cybershop.training) is the same one
    the Web Fundamentals and HTTP Deep Dive terminal missions, and every
    later websec-* interactive lab, already use.
    
    Wires lesson -> mission cross-links (all three lessons link to the real
    web-fundamentals terminal mission - core-concepts' Challenge exercise
    uses the open/headers terminal commands directly, unlike Networking's
    core-concepts, so it needed the link too) but withholds the
    free-practice terminal link this module's lessons would otherwise get:
    the bare /terminal sandbox never attaches a simulated web app to the
    shell (only MissionRunner._attach_web_lab does), so open/headers/
    cookies/response would fail there.
    
    New in this ticket: lesson -> lab cross-links, additive alongside the
    existing mission links. YC-036.4/.5 deliberately scoped to missions only
    and documented lab-linking as a real gap for whoever picked up the next
    module. Adds a small _LESSON_LAB_LINKS mapping (app/roadmap/services.py)
    and one new conditional block in lesson.html: core-concepts links to the
    real websec-http (HTTP Requests & Responses) lab, hands-on-practice
    links to the real websec-cookies (Cookie Security Flags) lab - both
    matched by actual content, not a generic guess.
    
    Roadmap structure, ordering, and lock (v1.0, YC-036.2) are unchanged per
    the freeze - only lesson content and additive, scoped cross-links.
    
    Adds TestWebFundamentalsContent to tests/test_roadmap_lock.py (mirrors
    TestNetworkingFundamentalsContent's discipline: real-content checks,
    taught-term checks, mission/lab link scoping, real-route checks, XP/order
    invariants, HTTP-level page renders, CyberMentor context) and makes the
    shared `app` fixture self-seed lab rows by name (seed_labs(), guarded by
    an idempotency check) so this test file no longer silently depends on
    another test module's collection order for its lab-link assertions.
    Updates docs/ROADMAP_LOCK.md's content-status tracking (87 -> 84 empty
    lessons) plus a documented list of topics intentionally deferred to a
    future pass (dedicated HTML/CSS/JS lessons, REST-as-theory, session
    attacks, WebSockets/HTTP2/HTTP3).
    
    Verified via full pytest suite (1705 passed x2) and ruff (no new
    violations - confirmed by diffing against the parent commit's version of
    every touched file). Manually walked all three lessons in-browser:
    content renders with no placeholder text, mission/lab links resolve to
    real routes, CyberMentor receives "Web Fundamentals — <lesson>" context,
    lesson completion awards the correct XP once (no duplication on
    refresh), and completing all three lessons correctly awarded the +175
    module bonus exactly once and unlocked the next module (Git & GitHub).
    
